<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net8.0</TargetFramework>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <LangVersion>12.0</LangVersion>
    <AssemblyName>VL.GeoJSON</AssemblyName>
    <!--
      The VL node category is derived from the NAMESPACE minus the "VL" prefix (see
      AssemblyInfo.cs), so this yields categories under "GeoJSON". Assembly name and root
      namespace happen to agree here, unlike in VL.NetTopologySuite where "NetTopologySuite"
      had to be abbreviated to "NTS" to stay readable in the node browser. "GeoJSON" is
      already short, so nothing needs decoupling - but the two are still set separately and
      deliberately, because that is the rule both sibling repositories follow.
    -->
    <RootNamespace>VL.GeoJSON</RootNamespace>
    <Version>0.0.1</Version>
    <Authors>VL.GeoJSON Contributors</Authors>
    <Description>GeoJSON as vvvv gamma nodes</Description>
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>

    <!--
      Put every NuGet dependency next to the built assembly. A class library does not do this
      by default: it emits VL.GeoJSON.dll alone and leaves the host to resolve
      NetTopologySuite from deps.json plus the NuGet cache.

      vvvv does not resolve it that way through a ProjectDependency, and the failure is quiet.
      It is also total for this package rather than partial: EVERY node here mentions an NTS
      or NTS.Features type in its signature, so an unresolvable NetTopologySuite means every
      node is greyed out - not red, not logged, no exception - and vvvvc still exits 0.
    -->
    <CopyLocalLockFileAssemblies>true</CopyLocalLockFileAssemblies>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      VL.Core supplies [ImportAsIs], [Name], [SkipCategory] and [ProcessNode].

      2025.7.2 is the floor, matching VL.NetTopologySuite: [Name] and [SkipCategory] arrived
      in gamma 7.2. ExcludeAssets="runtime" because VL.Core ships inside vvvv; shipping our
      copy is what every note in both sibling repositories warns against. Deliberately absent
      from the nuspec for the same reason.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="VL.Core" Version="2025.7.2" ExcludeAssets="runtime" />

    <!--
      2.6.0, the same version both sibling repositories pin. The whole NTS 2.x line carries
      assembly version 2.0.0.0, so a vvvv loading VL.GeoJSON, VL.NetTopologySuite and
      VL.Mapsui together sees ONE identity. That is load bearing rather than tidy: the reason
      VL.Mapsui and VL.GIS could not coexist for months was a shared library resolving to two
      incompatible versions in vvvv's flat, machine-wide %LOCALAPPDATA%\vvvv\gamma\nugets\.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="NetTopologySuite" Version="2.6.0" />

    <!--
      The neutral feature model - NetTopologySuite.Features.Feature, FeatureCollection and
      AttributesTable - rather than a VLFeature of our own.

      2.1.0 is not a free choice: it is what NetTopologySuite.IO.GeoJSON4STJ 4.0.0 resolves
      (measured 2026-08-15 with `dotnet list package`, transitive included) AND what
      VL.Mapsui already declares. Naming the same number in all three places means the
      hand-off needs no adapter and the loader sees one assembly.

      Declared explicitly although GeoJSON4STJ drags it in anyway: VL needs an upstream
      library to be a PACKAGE IN A PACKAGE REPOSITORY, not merely a restorable assembly.
      Without that it cannot resolve the type, and a node whose signature mentions Feature is
      built with no working pins while every link to it is dropped - vvvvc exits 0 and
      nothing is red. build.ps1 installs what the .vl declares into deps\.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="NetTopologySuite.Features" Version="2.1.0" />

    <!--
      GeoJSON4STJ rather than NetTopologySuite.IO.GeoJSON, and the reasons are measured
      rather than stylistic (2026-08-15, both at 4.0.0):

        - no Newtonsoft.Json dependency; System.Text.Json is in-box on net8.0
        - HALF the retained memory: 49 MB vs 101 MB for 50,000 polygons, 13.6 vs 25.4 MB
          for 10,000. Retained, not merely allocated - this is memory a patch holds for as
          long as the data is on screen
        - parse speed is a tie (122 ms vs 112 ms at 10,000 features; 629 vs 650 at 50,000)
        - it is the only one of the two with RingOrientationOption, which is what makes an
          exact polygon round trip possible at all

      The cost, and it is a real one: this package returns internal StjFeature objects that
      implement IFeature but are NOT NetTopologySuite.Features.Feature, behind an IMMUTABLE
      JsonElementAttributesTable. Defaults.Normalise deals with that; see its remarks.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="NetTopologySuite.IO.GeoJSON4STJ" Version="4.0.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
